+// escha_F is a pure function of a 16-bit codeword, so all 65,536 of its values fit in a table of
+// 128 KiB (two f16 results per uint). The estate CLOSED this option by reasoning - "a 128 KB random
+// lookup per weight on a bandwidth-shared APU loses to ~7 ALU ops" - and never ran it. That is an
+// unverified refusal, and the arithmetic behind it has changed twice since: the generator is now
+// about six operations, and this device carries 2 MiB of L2, so the whole table can sit resident.
+//
+// The access is not really random either: consecutive codewords come from one 32-bit word shifted
+// by two bits at a time, so neighbouring lookups land in nearby table entries.
+//
+// MEASURED AND REFUTED, 2026-08-21. The table is 4.9x SLOWER than the arithmetic:
+//     arithmetic generator   pp512 16.78   tg32 3.92
+//     65,536-entry table     pp512 16.62   tg32 0.80
+// Same binary, arms alternated, idle rig, Balanced. The estate had closed this option by reasoning
+// and the reasoning was right - it is now closed by a number instead. Prefill is untouched because
+// the multi-column path amortises the lookup across columns; decode, one lookup per weight, is
+// where it dies. Correctness was proven first: test-backend-ops -o ESCHA_MM, 20 cases, 0 FAIL on
+// BOTH arms, so this is a speed verdict on a correct implementation.
+//
+// Values are computed host-side with the SAME arithmetic, so the table is exact by construction,
+// not approximately equal. ESCHA_LUT=0 restores the arithmetic generator.
+#ifndef ESCHA_LUT
+#define ESCHA_LUT 0
+#endif
